doctype html
html(lang='en')

    - var base = '/'

    mixin stage(num, name, when, text, outcome)
        .guide-stage
            .guide-stage-num= num
            div
                h3.h5.mb-1= name
                p.small.text-primary.fw-bold.mb-2= when
                p.mb-2= text
                p.mb-0
                    strong By the end of the stage: 
                    | #{outcome}

    head

        include includes/head.pug

        meta(name='description', content="A parent's guide to learning to code at Spark Coding, Rouse Hill: the four stages from Scratch to Python, what your child can do after a term, a year and three years, and where coding can lead.")
        meta(property='og:type', content='article')
        meta(property='og:url', content='https://sparkcoding.com.au/guide')
        meta(property='og:title', content="Your Child's Coding Journey | A Parent's Guide | Spark Coding")
        meta(property='og:description', content='The four stages from first code to real projects, and how you\u2019ll know your child is on track.')
        meta(property='og:image', content='https://sparkcoding.com.au/assets/img/og-image.png')
        meta(property='og:locale', content='en_AU')
        link(rel='canonical', href='https://sparkcoding.com.au/guide')

        title Your Child's Coding Journey | A Parent's Guide | Spark Coding

    body#page-top

        include includes/nav.pug

        header.masthead.masthead-short
            .container.px-4.px-lg-5
                .row.justify-content-center.text-center
                    .col-lg-8
                        p.text-white-75.text-uppercase.fw-bold.small.mb-3 A parent's guide
                        h1.text-white.font-weight-bold Your Child's Coding Journey
                        hr.divider
                        p.text-white-75.mb-0 Coding is a long-term skill, like reading or music. This guide shows what the journey looks like at Spark Coding, from a child's first lines of code to the opportunities open to teenagers who keep at it.

        section.page-section.guide
            .container.px-4.px-lg-5
                .row.justify-content-center
                    .col-lg-8

                        .guide-glance.mb-5
                            h2.h5.mt-0 At a glance
                            ul.mb-0
                                li A clear, age-mapped path from Year 3 through Year 12.
                                li Personalised learning, with a maximum of 3 students per class.
                                li Optional doors along the way, like competitions, scholarships and real-project portfolios. None need to be decided up front.
                                li Honest about timing: most children spend one to two years at each stage.

                        h2#stages The stages of the journey
                        hr.divider.ms-0
                        p.text-muted Four stages, each building on the last. Older beginners often skip the earlier stages entirely.

                        +stage('1', 'First Code', 'Ages 8–9 · Year 3 · Scratch', 'Learning to think in code by building animations and simple games. Children move from playing games to making them, a quietly transformative shift in how they see technology.', 'can build a complete game with score-keeping, multiple levels, and characters that respond to the player.')
                        +stage('2', 'Bridge to Real Code', 'Ages 9–10 · Years 3–4 · MakeCode Arcade', 'Building arcade games with familiar blocks, then flipping to read the real Python code underneath. It\u2019s the first taste of what professional programmers see on screen, with the blocks they already know as a safety net. Games can be loaded onto a handheld micro:bit console.', 'can recognise the same code in two forms, blocks and Python, and make small text edits with confidence.')
                        +stage('3', 'Python', 'Ages 10–12 · Years 4–6 · Python', 'The same language used in real software and at university. Children write Python from scratch and build small, useful programs, like quizzes, calculators and word games, that they can show off to family and friends.', 'a portfolio of half a dozen working programs they wrote themselves, and the vocabulary to read code in any modern language.')
                        +stage('4', 'Building Skills', 'Ages 12+ · Year 7 onwards · Python', 'Bigger programs and more sophisticated games, with real data, searching, sorting and testing. Students write bots that battle in Spark Arena tournaments. Those who want it can prepare for national programming competitions.', 'a substantial portfolio of real projects. Whether to take coding further is fully in the student\u2019s hands.')

                        .row.g-4.mt-2.mb-5
                            .col-md-6
                                .guide-note.h-100
                                    h3.h6 A note for older beginners
                                    p.small.mb-0 If your child is starting in Year 5 or later, we don't rewind them through the earlier stages. A Year 7 beginner typically starts at Stage 3 (Python), building real programs from the very first lesson. The stages are a map, not a queue.
                            .col-md-6
                                .guide-note.h-100
                                    h3.h6 How we choose where to start
                                    p.small.mb-0 The 45-minute trial doubles as a placement check. We see what your child already knows, what excites them, and what project would feel meaningful to build. By the end of the trial, we'll have a clear, personalised plan for the next term.

                        h2#lead Where this can lead
                        hr.divider.ms-0
                        p.text-muted Three doors that open progressively. None are required. Your child can step through any, all, or none, and still come out a stronger thinker.

                        .guide-door
                            p.small.text-primary.fw-bold.mb-1 Door 1 · Every student
                            h3.h5 A stronger learner
                            p Computational thinking is now part of the NSW curriculum. Students who code think more clearly, debug their own work, and break big problems into smaller ones. Those skills show up in maths, science, and even essay writing.
                        .guide-door
                            p.small.text-primary.fw-bold.mb-1 Door 2 · From around Year 3, opt-in
                            h3.h5 Friendly national competitions
                            p
                                | Australia runs short online competitions every year that are fun, low-cost and designed to be enjoyable: 
                                strong Bebras
                                |  (Feb) and 
                                strong AMC
                                |  (Aug) from Year 3, 
                                strong CAT
                                |  (May) and the free 
                                strong Raspberry Pi Foundation Coding Challenge
                                |  (June) from Year 5, and from Year 7 the 
                                strong Australian Informatics Olympiad
                                | . We register and prepare students in class, with no extra work at home.
                        .guide-door.mb-5
                            p.small.text-primary.fw-bold.mb-1 Door 3 · From Year 7, requires sustained effort
                            h3.h5 Scholarships, university, and beyond
                            p.mb-0 For students who keep coding seriously into high school, sustained competition results are recognised by private-school scholarship panels, university bonus-point schemes, and international university applications. Just as importantly, the portfolio of real projects students build with us is a strong signal in its own right, from Year 9 work-experience applications through to university interviews.

                        h2#on-track How you'll know your child is on track
                        hr.divider.ms-0
                        .milestones.mb-5
                            .row.g-4
                                .col-md-4
                                    .milestone-when After one term
                                    p.mb-0 They'll show you a small program they built themselves. You'll start hearing them ask, "What if I made it do this instead?"
                                .col-md-4
                                    .milestone-when After one year
                                    p.mb-0 They'll fix their own bugs without needing to ask, and have made something they're genuinely proud of.
                                .col-md-4
                                    .milestone-when After three years
                                    p.mb-0 They'll read and write the same kind of code professional programmers use, have at least one competition certificate, and know whether coding is "a fun side thing" or something they want to keep doing.

                        h2#examples What the journey can look like
                        hr.divider.ms-0
                        p.text-muted Three illustrative examples (not real students). Each child starts at a different age and takes a different path.

                        .guide-example
                            h3.h6 Mia, starts in Year 3
                            p.small.mb-0 First year: builds her first Scratch animations and sits Bebras for fun. Year 4: makes her first arcade-style game and sits the AMC. Year 5: moves into Python, sits her first CAT, and builds a recipe-recommendation app for the school showcase. By Year 7 she's writing real programs and sitting her first Australian Informatics Olympiad.
                        .guide-example
                            h3.h6 Eli, starts in Year 6
                            p.small.mb-0 No coding experience, but wants a head start before high school. Spends his first term in Python building small, real programs like a quiz game and a Mad Libs generator. By the end of Year 7 he sits his first Australian Informatics Olympiad, and by Year 9 he's confident enough to help younger students.
                        .guide-example.mb-5
                            h3.h6 Aanya, starts in Year 4
                            p.small.mb-0 Does Scratch and the bridge-to-Python stage through Years 4 and 5, picks up Python in Year 6, and sits AMC and Bebras every year. When she sits a private-school scholarship at the end of Year 6, her competition results and portfolio of Python projects give her plenty to talk about. Whatever the outcome, she starts Year 7 confident and capable.

                        .guide-cta.text-center
                            h2.h4.mt-0 Ready to find your child's starting point?
                            p.mb-4 The 45-minute trial is $20, credited to your first term when you enrol. Classes are in person at 5 Glen Abbey St, Rouse Hill, with a maximum of three students at $59 per lesson, billed per term.
                            a.btn.btn-primary.btn-xl.me-sm-2.mb-2(href='/#trial-form') Book a $20 Trial
                            a.btn.btn-outline-primary.btn-xl.mb-2(href='tel:+61455666640', onclick='sparkReportCall()')
                                i.bi.bi-telephone-fill.me-2
                                | Call 0455 666 640

        include includes/footer.pug
